# Part 2: Testing Assumptions of Linear Models {#sec-p2_assumptions}

::: {.callout-note .partmenu #parts-0602}
## Sections
- @sec-independent
- @sec-continuous
- @sec-normality
- @sec-qq
- @sec-constant_variance
- @sec-res_fitted
- @sec-scale_location
- @sec-influential
- @sec-plotall
- @sec-summary_0602
:::

:::{.callout-tip .objectives #objectives-0602}
#### Learning objectives

By the end of this part of the practical you will be able to:

* Describe the main assumptions of linear models.
* Extract fitted values and residuals from a fitted linear model.
* Use diagnostic plots to assess normality, constant variance and linearity.
* Identify potentially influential observations in a linear model.
* Use R to generate standard diagnostic plots for a fitted linear model.
:::

In this part of the practical, you will need one Quarto document:

* `prac6_exercises.qmd` for basic exercises.
* `pollinators_assumptions.qmd` for exercises involving the PoMs pollinator dataset.

As we discussed in @sec-p2_hypothesis_testing_1, T-tests, ANOVA and linear regression are all based on the same general linear-model framework and therefore share many assumptions.

| Assumption | t-test | ANOVA | Linear regression |
|---|:---:|:---:|:---:|
| Independent observations | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ |
| Continuous response | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ |
| Normally distributed residuals | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ |
| Constant variance of residuals | ✓* | ✓ | ✓ |
| Linear relationship with continuous explanatory variables | — | — | ✓ |

* For a standard two-sample t-test, equal variance is assumed. Welch's t-test does not assume equal variance.

In Practical 4, we assumed the assumptions of all our models had been met, however, in reality we should always check this before carrying out a test.

## Independent observations {#sec-independent .core}

[**Core**]{.core-text}

[↑ top](#)

Each observation should be independent of the others. For example, repeated measurements from the same site should not usually be treated as independent observations. Whether observations are independent depends primarily on how the data were collected. This is a study design step rather than an analysis step.

## Continuous response {#sec-continuous .core}

[**Core**]{.core-text}

[↑ top](#)

The response variable should be continuous, e.g. mass, temperature, distance. Standard linear models assume that the response can vary continuously around the value predicted by the model. Binary responses, categorical responses and counts generally require different types of model.

## Normally distributed residuals {#sec-normality .core}

[**Core**]{.core-text}

[↑ top](#)

The **residuals** — the differences between the observed values and the values predicted by the model — should be approximately normally distributed.

Let's go back to our first linear model from Practical 4 (@sec-p2_hypothesis_testing_1) - for the hypothesis that minimum temperature is different at sites where the bee *H. tumulorum* is present than at sites where it is absent. 

We can read the data frame we applied in Practical 4 and refit the linear model.


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
# Read the presence / absence data frame
ht_df <- read_csv("data/ht_df.csv")

# Refit the linear model
linear_model_ht <- lm(min_temp_coldest_month ~ detected,
  data = ht_df,
)
print(summary(linear_model_ht))
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}

```

