As for the categorical data, we can extract our fitted and residual values from the model. In this case the fitted values are the values which would be on the line and the residuals are the distances between the black dots and the line.

::: {.callout-exercise #ex-temp_prec_diag .rec}

### Plot fitted vs residual

[**Recommended**]{.rec-text}

In your `pollinators.qmd` Quarto file:

* Create a diagnostic data frame for the `lm_temp_prec` fitted model using the function `get_diagnostic_stats()`.
* Plot a scatter plot with fitted values on the x axis and residual values on the y axis.
* Add a smoothed trend line to the scatter plot using the function `geom_smooth()` with the argument `se = FALSE`. This will draw a smoothed trend line (not a regression line) through the data, making systematic patterns easier to see. We previously used this function to draw a regression line by adding the argument `method = "lm"`, but this is not what we want here.

::: {.callout-answer collapse="true" #ex-temp_prec_diag_ans}

[Create a diagnostic data frame for the `lm_temp_prec` fitted model using the function `get_diagnostic_stats()`]{.blue-text}


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
temp_prec_diagnostics <- get_diagnostic_stats(lm_temp_prec)
gt(head(temp_prec_diagnostics))
```

