[Plot a scatter plot with fitted values on the x axis and residual values on the y axis.]{.blue-text}


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
ggplot(
  temp_prec_diagnostics,
  aes(x = fitted, y = residual)
) +
  geom_point() +
  geom_smooth(se = FALSE) +
  theme_bw()
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stderr}

```
`geom_smooth()` using method = 'loess' and formula = 'y ~ x'
```


:::

::: {.cell-output-display}
![](06-02_model_assumptions_files/figure-html/temp_prec_diag_ans2-1.png){width=672}
:::
:::

:::
:::

If the constant variance assumption is met, the vertical spread of the residuals should be roughly similar across the range of fitted values. Here, there is no obvious increase or decrease in the spread of the residuals, so the assumption of constant variance appears reasonable.

## Scale-Location {#sec-scale_location .rec}

[**Recommended**]{.rec-text}

[↑ top](#)

An alternative diagnostic is the Scale–Location plot. This plots the square root of the absolute standardised residuals against the fitted values. Standardised residuals express each residual relative to its expected variability, making residuals easier to compare with one another.

In some cases, this makes changes in residual variability easier to see.

We already calculated these values above using our `get_diagnostic_stats()` function - we extracted them from the model using the R function `rstandard()`, then applied the `abs()` function to get the absolute values, then the `sqrt()` function to get the square root.

We can now plot the square root of the absolute values of the standardised residuals against our fitted values.


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
ggplot(
  temp_prec_diagnostics,
  aes(x = fitted, y = sqrt_abs_standardised_residual)
) +
  geom_point() +
  geom_smooth(se = FALSE) +
  theme_bw()
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stderr}

```
`geom_smooth()` using method = 'loess' and formula = 'y ~ x'
```


:::

::: {.cell-output-display}
![](06-02_model_assumptions_files/figure-html/plot_diag_stand_temp_prec-1.png){width=672}
:::
:::


Again, this looks like the equal variance assumption has been met.

## Breaking the equal variance assumption {#sec-break_equal_variance .fold .extension}

[**Extension**]{.extension-text}

If the assumption of equal variance is not met, the plot will look a bit different.

For example, let's simulate a dataset where plant biomass is the response variable and age is the explanatory variable, and increase the standard deviation with increasing age. It is common in biological systems for the magnitude of the variability in a measurement to naturally increase as the expected value increases.


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
plant_data <- tibble(
  age = runif(200, 1, 10),
  biomass = 10 + 5 * age +
    rnorm(200, mean = 0, sd = age * 2)
)
```
:::


::: {.callout-exercise #ex_plant_biomass .extension}

### Breaking the equal variance assumption

[**Extension**]{.extension-text}

In your `prac6_exercises.qmd` Quarto file:

* Fit a linear model to this data.
* Make a diagnostic data frame based on the fitted model using the `get_diagnostic_stats()` function.
* Plot fitted against residual values, add a smoothed trend line to the plot.
* Plot fitted against the square root of the absolute standardised residual values, add a smoothed trend line to the plot.

::: {.callout-answer collapse="true" #ex_plant_biomass_ans}

[Fit a linear model to this data.]{.blue-text}


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
lm_plant_biomass <- lm(biomass ~ age, plant_data)
print(summary(lm_plant_biomass))
```

