We then plot our standardised residuals against leverage and use point size to represent Cook's distance.


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
ggplot(
  temp_prec_diagnostics_influ,
  aes(
    x = leverage,
    y = standardised_residual
  )
) +
  geom_point(aes(size = cooks_distance)) +
  theme_bw()
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![](06-02_model_assumptions_files/figure-html/temp_prec_diagnostics_influ_plot-1.png){width=672}
:::
:::


The largest points have the greatest Cook's distances and therefore have the greatest influence on the fitted model. There are several observations with relatively high leverage or relatively large residuals, but no single observation appears to have an exceptionally large influence on the model.

